  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nOverall trends have stayed on track regarding the forecast tonight. \nA weakening line of storms will begin to move into our northwestern \ncounties across the Miami Valley between 7-9 PM this evening. \nEffective shear of 25-30 kts will build into the region later today \nwith an H5 shortwave ejecting through the Ohio Valley. Additionally, \nmodest instability around 1500 J/kg will be available near the onset \nof thunderstorm activity, but these values will trend lower as we \nprogress through the evening hours. A few strong to severe storms \nremain possible late this evening for portions of the Miami Valley \nand southeast IN before storms become more elevated and weaken in \ncoverage/intensity as they progress southeast. Primary severe threat \nwill be damaging straight-line winds, with isolated large hail being \na secondary threat. \n\nSurface cold front will slowly progress through the area tonight \nwhich should keep chances for isolated showers/storms around. The \nfront will be near the I-71 corridor by the mid-moring hours tomorrow\nbefore finally exiting in the late afternoon/early evening. Thus, \nadditional convection ahead of the front could result in another \nround of strong storms, mainly for our far southeast in the lower \nScioto Valley and north-central KY. Thinking that the primary severe \nthreat will remain east of our CWA, but a couple of our counties may \nstill get clipped by a stronger storm. Note that an isolated\nshower/storm could still develop behind the cold front. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nLongwave ridge will build across a large portion of the eastern and\ncentral CONUS late this weekend and persist through at least the\nearly part of next week. This will result in an influx of hot and\nhumid air back into the Ohio Valley, with daytime highs back above\nseasonal normals.",
